<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">

<head>
    <!-- Google tag (gtag.js) -->
    <script async src="https://www.googletagmanager.com/gtag/js?id=G-8BXNGKCR1N"></script>
    <script>
        window.dataLayer = window.dataLayer || [];
        function gtag() { dataLayer.push(arguments); }
        gtag('js', new Date());

        gtag('config', 'G-8BXNGKCR1N');
    </script>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>AI Support Search- Knowledge Base UX · Dhruv Panchal</title>

    <!-- Fonts -->
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com">
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin>
    <link
        href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Space+Grotesk:wght@300;400;500&family=Space+Mono:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,400&family=Syncopate:wght@400;700&display=swap"
        rel="stylesheet">

    <!-- Icons -->
    <link href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/remixicon@3.5.0/fonts/remixicon.css" rel="stylesheet">

    <!-- CSS -->
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="../style.css">

    <!-- Lenis -->
    <script src="https://unpkg.com/@studio-freight/lenis@1.0.29/dist/lenis.min.js"></script>
</head>

<body>

    <!-- Nav -->

    <!-- Custom Cursor -->
    <div class="cursor-dot"></div>
    <div class="cursor-circle"></div>

    <!-- Noise Overlay -->
    <div class="noise-overlay"></div>

    <main id="main-content">

        <!-- Hero -->
        <header class="case-hero">
            <h1 class="case-title">Users spent 5 minutes searching for documents they knew existed- and gave up anyway.
                I redesigned the search to get them there in 30 seconds.</h1>
            <p class="case-subtitle">Replaced keyword-only search with AI-powered semantic matching- turning the support
                portal's self-service from a frustration into the default path for users.</p>

            <div class="case-meta">
                <div class="meta-item">
                    <span class="label">Role</span>
                    <span class="value">UI/UX Designer & UI Developer</span>
                </div>
                <div class="meta-item">
                    <span class="label">Duration</span>
                    <span class="value">Multi-month Project</span>
                </div>
                <div class="meta-item">
                    <span class="label">Output</span>
                    <span class="value">AI-powered Search Interface</span>
                </div>
            </div>
        </header>

        <div class="case-image-wrapper">
            <img src="../case-study-img/ai-search/SmartSearch%20Cover%20Screen.png" alt="AI Support Portal Search UI"
                class="case-image">
        </div>

        <!-- Content Structure -->
        <article class="case-content">

            <!-- 1. Problem Statement -->
            <section class="case-section">
                <h2 class="section-heading">Problem Statement</h2>
                <p class="body-text">
                    Users were searching the support portal for documents they knew existed- and
                    leaving empty-handed. The existing keyword search matched only on exact document
                    titles. A user searching 'my payment didn't go through' got no results. The
                    document they needed was titled 'Payment Failure Error Resolution'- close in
                    meaning, zero match in keywords.
                </p>
                <p class="body-text" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
                    Every failed self-service attempt became a support ticket or a call to a Strategic
                    Account Manager. SAMs were answering questions a working search would have resolved
                    in under a minute.
                </p>
            </section>

            <!-- 2. Validation (WHY) -->
            <section class="case-section">
                <h2 class="section-heading">Why this mattered</h2>
                <p class="body-text">This was a real problem because:</p>
                <ul class="clean-list">
                    <li>Most users didn’t know the exact document titles or locations.</li>
                    <li>Search was frustrating, slow, and often led to dead ends.</li>
                    <li>Users wasted time, or abandoned self-service, increasing pressure on SAMs and support teams.
                    </li>
                    <li>Every ticket raised for basic information created extra workload and slowed down resolution for
                        everyone.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <!-- 3. Hypothesis -->
            <section class="case-section">
                <h2 class="section-heading">Hypothesis</h2>

                <h3 class="subsection-title">What we concluded</h3>
                <ul class="clean-list">
                    <li>The main problem was strict search that relied on exact matches.</li>
                    <li>Users needed a search that understood intent, not just keywords.</li>
                    <li>The UI made it hard to skim or filter results.</li>
                </ul>

                <br>

                <h3 class="subsection-title">What we did</h3>
                <p class="body-text"><strong>Result: users now find answers in under 30 seconds- down from 5 minutes or
                        more.</strong></p>
                <ul class="clean-list" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
                    <li>Integrated AI-powered, semantic search to deliver more human results.</li>
                    <li>Redesigned the search UI for faster scanning and easier filtering.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>


            <!-- 4. Process -->
            <section class="case-section">
                <h2 class="section-heading">Process</h2>

                <div class="timeline-container">

                    <!-- Explore -->
                    <div class="timeline-item">
                        <div class="timeline-marker"></div>
                        <div class="timeline-content">
                            <h3 class="timeline-title">Explore</h3>
                            <p class="body-text">
                                Looked at options: keyword-only search, advanced filters, and AI/semantic search.
                                Explored different layouts: list, card, and grouped results.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Iterate -->
                    <div class="timeline-item">
                        <div class="timeline-marker"></div>
                        <div class="timeline-content">
                            <h3 class="timeline-title">Iterate</h3>
                            <p class="body-text">
                                Tested two result layouts with internal users on timed tasks. In the card layout,
                                users spent ~8 seconds scanning each result before selecting. In the list layout
                                with keyword highlights, that dropped to ~3 seconds. Decision: list wins. Cards
                                looked cleaner- lists worked faster. In a support context where users are already
                                frustrated, speed beats aesthetics.
                            </p>
                            <p class="body-text" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
                                Filter design: started with 8 filter options (content type, product area, date,
                                author, version, format, region, audience). Users engaged with 2 of them consistently.
                                Cut to 2 filters. Removed the rest.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Implement -->
                    <div class="timeline-item">
                        <div class="timeline-marker"></div>
                        <div class="timeline-content">
                            <h3 class="timeline-title">Implement</h3>
                            <p class="body-text">
                                Went with a clean list layout, with key info and highlights. Added a “Did you mean”
                                feature for mistyped queries and integrated semantic AI search to handle broad, natural
                                questions. Worked under a tight 1-month timeline and with legacy data structures.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="case-section">
                <h2 class="section-heading">Designing for when the AI gets it wrong</h2>
                <p class="body-text">
                    AI search errors in a support context destroy trust faster than no AI search at all.
                    A confident wrong answer is worse than "I don't know." Three decisions addressed this:
                </p>
                <ul class="clean-list" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
                    <li><strong>Source attribution over confidence scores:</strong> Instead of "87% confidence"
                        (meaningless to users), every result shows "Based on: [Article name]" with a direct link. Users
                        can verify. This builds more trust than a percentage.</li>
                    <li><strong>Fallback always visible:</strong> "Contact our team →" appears below every result set,
                        never hidden. Users should never feel trapped by the AI result.</li>
                    <li><strong>Honest low-confidence state:</strong> When AI confidence is low, the interface says "We
                        found related articles, but your specific question may need a human"- not a hallucinated answer.
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <!-- 5. Screenshots -->
            <section class="case-section">
                <h2 class="section-heading">Screenshots & Notes</h2>


                <div class="case-image-wrapper">
                    <img src="../case-study-img/ai-search/global-before.jpg" alt="New Search Page"
                        class="case-image-full">
                    <p class="image-caption text-center">Old keyword search- exact title match only. A query like
                        'payment didn't work' returned zero results despite a relevant article existing in the knowledge
                        base.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="case-image-wrapper">
                    <img src="../case-study-img/ai-search/globalsearchcurrent.jpg" alt="AI Powered Results"
                        class="case-image-full">
                    <p class="image-caption text-center">New AI-powered search- semantic matching returns relevant
                        results for natural language queries, with keyword highlights in excerpts and source attribution
                        on every result.</p>
                </div>
            </section>


            <!-- 6. Solution -->
            <section class="case-section">
                <h2 class="section-heading">Final Solution</h2>
                <ul class="clean-list">
                    <li>Switched to a smarter, AI-powered search that understands user questions, not just keywords.
                    </li>
                    <li>Reduced clutter and made the search results easier to scan and filter.</li>
                    <li>Added suggestions and highlights to guide users.</li>
                    <li>Users now find what they need in less than 30 seconds, down from 5 minutes or more.</li>
                    <li>Fewer support tickets for basic document requests.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <!-- 7. Impact -->
            <section class="case-section">
                <h2 class="section-heading">Impact & Learnings</h2>
                <h3 class="subsection-title">Impact & ROI</h3>
                <p class="body-text">
                    By making self-service actually work, we didn't just improve the interface - we recovered massive
                    operational costs and user time. Tracking the metrics post-launch revealed a compelling story of
                    business value:
                </p>
                <ul class="clean-list" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
                    <li><span><strong>Search Time: 10× Faster</strong>- Time to find a document dropped from <strong>5+
                                minutes</strong> to <strong>under 30 seconds</strong>.</span></li>
                    <li><span><strong>Support Ticket Deflection: 68% Reduction</strong>- "Can't find document" support
                            tickets practically vanished in the first four weeks.</span></li>
                    <li><span><strong>High Deflection Rate:</strong> The combination of semantic search and "Did you
                            mean" logic successfully resolved <strong>94%</strong> of search journeys without requiring
                            user escalation.</span></li>
                    <li><span><strong>CSAT Score Transformation:</strong> User satisfaction with portal search jumped
                            from a dismal <strong>1.8/5</strong> to <strong>4.7/5</strong>.</span></li>
                </ul>

                <h4
                    style="font-family: var(--font-heading); color: #22C55E; font-size: 1.1rem; text-transform: uppercase; margin: 3rem 0 1rem;">
                    The Business Calculation</h4>
                <blockquote style="border-left: 2px solid #22C55E; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0 0 3rem 0;">
                    <p class="body-text"
                        style="font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; color: var(--color-muted);">
                        <strong style="color: var(--color-accent);">User Productivity Reclaimed:</strong><br>
                        10,000 searches/mo &times; 4.5 mins saved = 45,000 mins/mo<br>
                        <span style="color:#22C55E; font-weight:700;">&rarr; 750 hours saved per month</span> for our
                        users.
                    </p>
                    <p class="body-text"
                        style="font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 0; color: var(--color-muted);">
                        <strong style="color: var(--color-accent);">Support OpEx Reduction:</strong><br>
                        ~400 basic tickets averted/mo &times; $18 avg. cost/ticket<br>
                        <span style="color:#22C55E; font-weight:700;">&rarr; $7,200 saved per month</span> locally,
                        freeing SAMs to focus on high-value support instead of manual routing.
                    </p>
                </blockquote>

                <h3 class="subsection-title">Learnings</h3>
                <p class="body-text">
                    <strong>1. Speed beats aesthetics when users are frustrated.</strong><br>
                    During testing, a beautiful card layout lost to a denser, highlight-driven list layout. When users
                    are looking for support, they want to scan, not admire. Cutting 6 excess filters and focusing purely
                    on relevancy yielded a substantially better completion rate.
                </p>
                <p class="body-text" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
                    <strong>2. AI needs to admit when it's wrong.</strong><br>
                    A confident hallucination destroys trust faster than no search at all. Shifting to explicit source
                    attribution (<em>"Based on: Payment Guide"</em>) and providing prominent human fallbacks preserved
                    user confidence even when the search didn't yield a perfect answer.
                </p>
            </section>

        </article>

        <!-- Footer Nav -->
        <div class="case-footer-nav"
            style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 1rem;">
